course: dictionary
lesson: 12951
title: Excessive: too much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Excessive is an adjective. It means too much, more than is needed or sensible. A quick check: if you can swap in «too much» and the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning.
explain: «Eating excessive sugar is bad for your teeth.» The clue is bad for your teeth. A little sugar is not the problem, so excessive must mean more sugar than is good for you.
explain: Excessive can also mean going beyond what is fair or allowed. «The driver was fined for excessive speed.» A fine is given when a rule is broken, and the rule is a speed limit, so excessive speed means going faster than is allowed.
explain: In everyday talk people say «too much» or «way too much» instead. You may meet excessive in formal writing and exams, so learn it now. Its comparative form is more excessive, as in «his reaction was more excessive than hers».
fact: Excessive means too much, more than is needed or sensible.
fact: Excessive can also mean beyond what is fair or allowed, as in «excessive speed».
fact: In health lessons, «excessive sugar» and «excessive screen time» are harmful.
fact: Excessive is an adjective, so it describes a noun: «excessive noise» or «excessive heat».
fact: Swap test: if «too much» fits in the sentence, excessive has the same meaning.
exercise: «She spent excessive time on her phone.» What does excessive mean here? | Not enough | Just the right amount | *Too much | Quietly
exercise: «The driver was fined for excessive speed.» Why was the driver fined? | He forgot his coat | He was late for school | He drove too slowly to reach town | *He went faster than was allowed
exercise: «The excessive heat made everyone tired.» Find and copy the word that means too much. | tired | everyone | heat | *excessive
test: Which everyday phrase means the same as excessive? | *Way too much | Very quiet | Just enough | Not very bad
test: «Excessive screen time can make your eyes tired.» What is this sentence warning you about? | Screens that are broken | *Too much time looking at screens | Using screens only at school | Eyes that are very bright
exam: Which sentence uses excessive correctly? | She drank a glass of water. | We walked to the park. | The sky was blue. | *He ate excessive amounts of cake and felt ill.
